 {# Key Takeaways #}
 <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-xl p-6 mb-8">
 <p class="text-lg font-black uppercase text-amber-700 mb-3">Key Takeaways</p>
 <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm mb-4 speakable">GaN V is the current production standard: 40% smaller, 30% less heat, 65W BOM $4.80-6.50 at MOQ 500 (silicon $3.20-4.50); 500 units private-label, 3,000+ for new tooling.</p>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>GaN V chargers are 40% smaller and generate 30% less heat</strong> than equivalent silicon chargers, enabling 240W in a palm-sized enclosure. Standby power drops to &lt;0.1W vs 0.5W+ for silicon designs.</li>
 <li><strong>OEM BOM cost for a 65W GaN V charger: $4.80-6.50/unit at MOQ 500</strong>, compared to $3.20-4.50 for silicon. The $1.60-2.00 gap is offset by lower shipping cost, fewer returns, and 20-30% retail premium.</li>
 <li><strong>3 non-negotiable manufacturer verification points</strong>: (1) verify GaN FET part numbers on the production BOM, not just the prototype; (2) confirm the factory owns their PCBA design or has a licensed reference design from Navitas/Infineon; (3) request a 72-hour full-load aging test report for your specific SKU.</li>
 <li><strong>MOQ for custom GaN V chargers: 500 units</strong> for private-label on in-stock models; 3,000+ for new tooling. Tooling cost: $2,000-5,000 for a single-port mold, 25-35 day production lead time after sample approval.</li>
 <li><strong>GaN V is the current production standard</strong>, not a future roadmap. Over 20% of new USB-C charger designs shipped in H1 2026 used GaN V FETs. Factories still quoting GaN I or GaN III as "latest" are running end-of-life inventory.</li>
 </ul>
